C(40,[58],'stated without proof','Setfi=πi andλi=χ in the functional formula. Both the tensor and scalar generators giveπ1*π2=χπ. No cancellation is needed. Whenχ=0, division byχ cannot uniquely recover a map.','C','The integral identity holds. The notation1/χ is justified either whenχ is invertible or as a mnemonic for an independently constructed quotient; it is not a general uniqueness theorem. Related torsor constructions do not remove this2-torsion issue.',['Loos1997','Pirashvili2019'],['Q04'])
C(41,[60],'false as stated','The preceding restriction computes(2χ−χ)λ1λ2u1u2=χλ1λ2u1u2. The isolated equation2−χ=1 has residual1−χ and fails atχ=0 overZ. Exact coefficient check in extension_checks.json.','F','The surrounding general formula is valid; the isolated line may contain a missing factor or belong to an abandoned normalization. No assumptionχ=1 is imported.',['Loos1997'],['Q04'])
C(42,[61],'open on the pages','IfF11 gives a morphism of the two short exact rows with identity endpoint maps, injectivity and surjectivity of its middle map follow by the short-five-lemma argument. This does not constructF11.','E','The conditional isomorphism implication is standard exact-sequence algebra. Existence of a compatible middle block remains the substantive problem; later χ=2 product coherence is narrower.',['SGA1','Loos1997'],['Q04'])
C(43,[62],'stated without proof','Under the reconstructionPi=a_iπ_i, Pi²=χPi. Atχ=2,γ=1 the candidate2I−P1−P2+P1P2 equalsI+(I−P1)(I−P2), henceI+σ1⊗σ2. The operator identity is exact. The four boundary squares must still be checked with their actual identifications.','C','This specializes to the familiar symmetrizer in the quadratic case. The manuscript’s broader candidate and conditional existence should not be promoted to a proved canonical strict comparison for arbitraryχ. Pirashvili sharesγχ=2 under additional torsor hypotheses.',['Loos1997','Pirashvili2019'],['Q04'],confidence='medium: candidate operators reconstructed from doubtful primed notation')
C(44,[63],'stated without proof','Withπa=2id, setσ=I−aπ. Thenσ²=I−2aπ+aπaπ=I. Conversely, ifσ induces−id onL andid onM, I−σ uniquely factors througha; its restriction givesπa=2id. Thus involutivity is automatic from these endpoint conditions. Split matrix checked in extension_checks.json; general exact-sequence proof is in proofs.md.','E','The integral equivalence is elementary and valid without2 being invertible. It is not a decomposition into eigenspaces, and the χ=2 case is meaningful in characteristic2.',['Loos1997','Pirashvili2019'],['Q04'])
C(45,[64,66],'false as stated','Take any nonzero field,χ=1,E_i=k m_i⊕kℓ_i,L_i=kℓ_i,π_i(m_i)=0,π_i(ℓ_i)=ℓ_i. The boundary imposes m1*ℓ2=0. The shear f1(m1)=m1+ℓ1,f1(ℓ1)=ℓ1 preservesL1 but maps that relation toℓ1⊗ℓ2≠0. Every stated broad hypothesis is met.','C','The broad endomorphism proposal fails. Adding compatibilityπ_i′f_i=α_iπ_i repairs the pushout descent, as in CL-SA02-031. The counterexample is conditional on the uncertain initial letter beingf_i and the displayed hypotheses being complete.',['Loos1997','Voight2016'],['Q04'],confidence='high for the counterexample; conditional textual scope')
C(46,[69],'stated without proof','The polynomial presentation makesA1⊗A2 commutative and locally free of rank4. Its centre is itself. Over a nonzero base, it cannot have centreR as a degree-two central Azumaya algebra would. This is a rank/centre obstruction, not a dimension analogy.','E','The earlier-run comparison correctly rules out identifying this commutative tensor algebra with a quaternion Azumaya algebra. Quaternion/conic theory requires additional different data.',['Voight2021'],['Q00'],statement='Earlier-run exclusion: the manuscript’s commutative rank-four tensor algebra is not a central degree-two Azumaya algebra over a nonzero base.')
C(47,[71],'stated without proof','Opened Voight chapter5: throughoutcharF≠2. Cor5.2.6 and5.5.2 identify quaternion isomorphism classes, ternary similarity classes and smooth conics; Th5.6.8 is an equivalence using oriented ternary forms and isomorphisms. Cor5.6.12 givesAut(B)=B×/F×. No object on97–145 supplies this comparison.','E','This is established external comparison material, not a theorem in the manuscript. The split identification withPGL2 applies after choosing the quaternion/ternary object and the stated field hypotheses.',['Voight2021'],['Q00'],dates='Book publication2021; no priority date for the classical correspondence is asserted.',statement='Earlier-run comparison only: over a field of characteristic different from2, quaternion algebras correspond to smooth conics and suitable nondegenerate ternary forms, with orientations needed for the categorical equivalence.')
C(48,[81],'stated without proof','No computation by Grothendieck is supplied here. Deligne’s Theorem1.6, WeilI p276, is opened: for smooth projectiveX/Fq, Frobenius onH^i has integral characteristic polynomial independent ofℓ≠p and all complex conjugate eigenvalues have absolute valueq^(i/2).','B','The recalled proof of the Weil conjectures is Deligne’s established result. Its proof does not establish the full standard conjectures. Grothendieck’s assessment of Deligne and the double-negative wording remain historical testimony.',['Deligne1974','GrothendieckRS'],['Q05'],dates='Deligne introduction attests Cambridge lecturesJuly1973; received1973-09-20; published1974-12-28. No stronger first-circulation claim is made.',statement='The mathematical content of the recollection is that Deligne proved the Weil conjectures; this is not an authorial proof on the reused leaf.')
C(49,[82,83],'open on the pages','No proof is claimed. Opened Grothendieck1969 pp195–198: algebraicity of Lefschetz operators and Künneth projectors, and Hodge-type positivity are distinct standard conjectures with quantified implications. B(X)+Hdg(X×X) implies the Weil estimate in his formulation.','C','The full standard-conjecture programme remains unresolved in the generality discussed. Agugliaro arXiv2510.21562 Cor1.3 proves the Hodge-type conjecture for powers of abelian threefolds over any field, a specific advance rather than the whole programme. The1969 conclusion explicitly pairs these conjectures with resolution, matching the recollection.',['Grothendieck1969','Agugliaro2025','GrothendieckRS'],['Q05'],dates='Bombay talk1968, proceedings1969; Agugliaro first arXiv2025-10-24, no later journal publication verified as of2026-11-19.',statement='For smooth projective varieties with a Weil cohomology, the standard conjectures ask for algebraicity of specified cohomological operators/projectors and positivity on primitive algebraic cycles. The prose asks about their validity collectively, not a new formulation or a proof.')
C(50,[83],'open on the pages','No resolution argument occurs on the leaf. Opened Cossart–Piltant Th1.1: every reduced separated Noetherian quasi-excellent scheme of dimension≤3 has a proper birational regular model, isomorphic over its regular locus. Opened deJong Th4.1 permits alterations, hence finite function-field extensions.','C','General positive-characteristic resolution is still open in the surveyed primary literature. Three-dimensional birational resolution and arbitrary-dimensional alterations are major restricted results; alterations do not equal birational resolution. The manuscript gives no narrower precise version to attribute.',['Grothendieck1969','CossartPiltant2019','deJong1996','Berczi2026'],['Q05'],dates='deJong received1995-06-28, publication1996; Cossart–Piltant arXivv1=2014-12-02,v2=2019-01-08, journal2019; Bérczi preprint2026-02.',statement='Resolution of singularities is named as a fundamental open question, without hypotheses on the base or a choice between embedded, birational, functorial or alteration formulations. A present-day precise comparison must therefore keep these variants separate.')
C(51,[39],'stated without proof','For genuine quadratic inputs,B is finite as anR-module, hence finite overB0. For spectrum surjectivity, pass to an algebraically closed residue field: if every input is étale, the map is the surjective parity map on eight points; otherwiseB0 has one point andB is nonempty. This proves surjectivity on every geometric fibre, hence globally. TheF2dual-number example proves nonflatness.','C','Finiteness and spectrum surjectivity of this product map are established by the elementary fibre argument here. They must not be confused with injectivity ofB0→B or flatness; the two independent transcriptions differ in the word at122.',['Loos1997','SGA1','Stacks04HN'],['Q01','Q03'],statement='The universal map Spec(A1⊗A2⊗A3)→Spec(A1∧A2∧A3) for genuine quadratic algebras is finite and surjective, but need not be flat.')
C(52,[17,20],'stated without proof','For the strong definition with n a multiplicative homogeneous degree-two law and t=polar_n(1,−), its characteristic polynomial isX²−t(x)X+n(x). The manuscript’s norm equation is precisely its image vanishing inA. Conversely this comparison does not recover the missing multiplicativity in the weaker recap.','C','Chenevier’s §1.19 supplies the contemporary degree-two Cayley–Hamilton language. This is a bounded mathematical translation, newly made in this run, not a documented historical influence or an identification of the weaker definition.',['Roby1963','Chenevier2014'],['Q02'],statement='Current-run comparison: the strong, universally multiplicative quasi-quadratic data form a commutative degree-two Cayley–Hamilton algebra in the polynomial-law sense; the weaker five-axiom list does not suffice.')
C(53,[67],'open on the pages','The local cocycle checks the overlap equation, but a symmetric monoidal structure also requires associator, symmetry and unit constraints and their diagrams. Invertible objects require a separate criterion. No implication fromγχ=2 to existence of the entire product is used.','C','Loos1997 Prop2.10 proves the symmetric tensor structure for graded quadratic algebras; Pirashvili gives categorical torsor structures withpq+2=0 and nondegeneracy. Neither exact theorem is the unspecified all-module/all-χ product package of the unavailable Hua register.',['Loos1997','Pirashvili2019'],['Q04'],statement='The worksheets give local product and transition formulas and attempt a comparison of constructions. Full global coherence and the Picard subcategory for arbitrary χ-extensions are separate tasks, not proved merely by these local calculations.')
C(54,[55,57],'stated without proof','For a fixed pair(fi,λi), the functional product is defined by CL-SA02-038. Ifλi is not uniquely determined, changing it changes the displayed tensor prescription; a scalar-restriction map alone therefore needs faithfulness or chosen scalar data.','C','The invertible-line case is compatible with the standard quadratic product, while the general-module extension requires the additional scalar data explicitly noted here. No exact published all-module variant was identified.',['Loos1997'],['Q04'],statement='The functional-product construction is well typed on pairs(fi,λi) satisfyingfi|Li=λiid, or on maps when eachLi is faithful; the unfinished Hom! notation alone does not settle that choice.')
C(55,[15,18],'proved there','Forx, n(σx)=n(x) as in CL-SA02-013. Forx,y,φ(x,y)=t(x)t(y)−t(xy) follows by expandingt(xσy); polarization of CH then gives the multiplication formula. These are universal quadratic identities, so arbitrary base change is handled by Roby PropII.1 rather than point sampling.','E','The computational identities are valid with the stated scalar polarization axiom. The finite Z/4 check is supplemented by an explicit universal law and does not substitute for it.',['Roby1963','Chenevier2014'],['Q02'],statement='The quasi-quadratic involution and polarization manipulations used on108–110 are identities of quadratic laws under the stated axioms, not merely functions on a finite set of base-ring points.')
C(56,[36],'proved there','For finite nonempty transitiveΓ-setJ, aΓ-stable closed subscheme of∐JSpecR is given by idealsIj all equalI. Its global ring is∏J R/I. The kernel ofR→∏J R/I isI; if this map is injective,I=0.','E','This is the corrected elementary lemma used in CL-SA02-027. It refines the false literal target and adds the closedness actually required by the ideal proof. The manuscript transcription itself is unchanged.',['SGA1'],['Q03'],statement='Refined statement of CL-SA02-028: a Γ-stable closed subscheme of a finite transitive constant Γ-scheme overR is the whole scheme ifR injects into its own global functions, not merely those of the ambient scheme.')

def save():
 (P/'claims.json').write_text(json.dumps(claims,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
